Follow the shadow setting for the popups of the GTK sheets

QtCurve gives a drop shadow to a menu, a tooltip and a combo box list.
The shadowSize key sets the reach of that shadow. The GTK 3 sheet held
one fixed token of 6 pixels of blur and a black ink. No rule of either
sheet read the size key. A user who widened the shadow saw no change,
and the sheet of GTK 4 gave a popup no shadow at all.

Both dialects now carry one popup rule that the two keys drive.
qtcX11ShadowInit() mixes a grey of 102 beside the window, and its
gradient falls to zero across the shadowSize key. A probe fits the
Gaussian blur of CSS to that curve. The blur is 0.220 of the key and
the spread is 0.088 of it, at a root mean square error of 0.036.

GTK 3 carries the rule on the decoration node of the popup window,
with a margin that holds the blur and the spread together. GTK 4
carries it on the contents node of the popover. The shipped theme of
that toolkit gives a popup shadow on the same node. It gives a tooltip
none, so the tooltip node of GTK 4 stays as it is. A false key holds
the property at none in both dialects, and the window keeps its own
token.

assert-shadow.sh reads six sheets over three arms of the settings. It
derives the pixel figures from the size key. It reads the grey, the
alpha, the room and the window token, and 19 doctored copies must fail
it.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
thirteenth commit. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !15
on system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

